WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.310
Early ocean-salt estimates counted salt entering the ocean but omitted important

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.530
salt removal. Kelvin calculated cooling before radioactive heating was understood.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.510
Both approaches produced ages far too young.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:15.870
The lesson is about model completeness:

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.710
a seemingly reasonable clock can fail if a major source or

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:20.590
sink is missing.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:25.290
Tree rings, ice layers and lake varves can preserve repeated seasonal

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:29.090
cycles. A varve is an annual sedimentary pair, not one year

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:30.870
for every light or dark stripe.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.010
Ice records can be checked with dated volcanic ash.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.970
These records demonstrate long durations, while each has its own preservation

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:38.950
and counting limits.

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:44.790
Monitored erosion rates and satellite measurements of plate motion provide another

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:49.110
scale of evidence. Centimeters of annual movement become large distances over

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:53.730
millions of years. Such comparisons require assumptions about past rates and

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.730
are not exact clocks for every landscape.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:01:00.910
Their value comes from agreement with independent geological relationships and isotopic

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:01.210
ages.

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.970
The supplied section describes Canadian rocks around four billion years old

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.330
and Australian zircon grains around four point four billion years old.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:12.630
A grain can survive erosion and enter a younger host.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:17.310
Earth’s surface also recycles older rocks through burial, metamorphism and melting,

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.710
so the oldest surviving rock need not date the planet’s formation.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:24.810
Meteorites and Moon rocks supply evidence beyond Earth’s recycled surface.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:29.350
The supplied textbook places solar-system formation between four point five three

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.870
and four point five eight billion years ago, summarized as an

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:35.310
Earth age of four point five five billion years.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:39.250
Consistent results across samples and methods support deep time;

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:41.530
the argument does not depend on one specimen.
